  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 415 AM CDT Thu Jun 25 2026\n\nCurrently, northwest flow aloft with a S/WV ridge to our west\nand surface high pressure over the Dakotas. Lingering boundary\nlayer moisture and weak sfc flow may again result in patchy fog\nthis morning from northwest North Dakota southeast through the\nJames River Valley. Also, low clouds are once again expected\nover eastern North Dakota including the James Valley this\nmorning, and already seeing these expanding in coverage there.\n\nS/WV mid level ridge approaches and builds across the Northern \nPlains today, resulting in mainly dry weather and a slight\nuptick in daytime highs compared to Wednesday, but overall still\nbelow normal. Morning clouds across the eastern third of the\nstate will contribute to a few diurnally driven showers/isold\nthunderstorms over my far north central and northeast this\nafternoon. S/WV mid level impulse moves across Montana and\neastward into the Northern Plains tonight, and will bring an\narea of showers and a few thunderstorms from west to east across\nportions of western and central ND through Friday morning, with\nthis activity lifting to the northeast Fri afternoon. Severe \nthreat is low with the convection associated with this wave \nwith limited instability expected.\n\nLarge upper level low slides southeast along the western \nCanadian coast today and tonight, then moves just off the Pacific\nNorthwest coastal area Fri/Friday night. Low amplitude long wave\ntrough over the western CONUS amplifies as a result,\ntransitioning our flow aloft to southwesterly Friday night.\nConvection is expected to initiate along a High Plains sfc \ntrough Fri aft/eve, then expanding north-northeast through Fri\nnight. Nocturnal low level jet (LLJ) may enhance convection over\ncentral/portions of eastern ND late Fri night as an instability\nplume lifts into the region. Right now SPC has southwest ND in\nthe Marginal Outlook, so will be interesting if this expands in\nfuture outlook updates. \n\nOver the weekend, southwest flow aloft increases as the large \nupper level low develops over much of the western CONUS within a\nlong wave trough regime. Models continue to show robust \ninstability and favorable shear over the state on Saturday, as \nwarm air and moisture advection increase along and ahead of a \ndeepening sfc trough across the far western Dakotas. Severe \nweather scenarios look to be either convection to our west \nmoving east into the area, and/or possible discrete convection \nalong the sfc trough/frontal boundary firing up. The former \nwould see wind and hail as main threats, with the latter seeing \nall facets of severe weather possible. SPC has pretty much all \nof western and central North Dakota in the Slight Risk (level 2 \nof 5) area for Day 3 (Saturday). \n\nFor Sunday, south to north orientated sfc trough/frontal \nboundary becomes quasi-stationary/redevelops over the central \nDakotas, leading to another round of strong to severe convection\nalong to east of this boundary. SPC has central and eastern\nareas of the state highlighted in the Day 4 outlook for this. \nIn addition to the active weather pattern, forecast temperatures\nthis coming weekend will warm to near to above normal for late \nJune, with forecast highs in the upper 70s/mid 80s and lows in \nthe mid 50s/low 60s. A few lower 90s possible out ahead of the\nsfc trough in the southern James Valley on Sunday. \n\nWe trend slightly cooler and drier for Monday as the closed low\nlifts northeast through the Northern Plains. Ahead of this\noccurrence, may see another round of strong convection during\nthe day across the local region depending on how fast the sfc \ntrough ejects east. \n\nThereafter, we remain in a southwest flow aloft pattern, with\nensembles trying to build upper level high pressure back west\nand north into the region, displacing the upper trough farther\nwest later in the week. With this flow pattern, NBM and \nensembles favor a nice warming trend next week to well above \nnormal temperatures, with forecast highs by next week Thursday \nwell into the 80s and lower 90s. This pattern would also yield \nnear daily thunderstorm chances, but more conditional and \ndependent on convective inhibition, any nocturnal LLJ, or any \nembedded impulses moving through.",
